// X64PrologueEpilogue — inserts frame setup/teardown ops after register
// allocation. EVERY function gets a REAL FRAME POINTER: the prologue opens with
// `push rbp` / `mov rbp, rsp`, then pushes the callee-saved GPRs the coloring
// used, reserves the frame with `sub rsp, N`, and finally stores the callee-saved
// XMMs it used into frame slots; the epilogue mirrors all four before each `ret`.
// This pass owns ALL of it: the return lowering emits only the R8 move + `ret`.
//
// ⭐ TWO FILES, TWO MECHANISMS, AND THE SPLIT IS FORCED BY THE ISA. A callee-saved GPR is
// preserved with `push`/`pop`; a callee-saved XMM cannot be, because **there is no `push xmm`
// in x86-64**. So xmm6–15 get their own ordered list (`calleeSavedXmmOrder`) and a
// dedicated frame SLOT each, saved and restored with the SAME `storeSlotReg`/`loadRegSlot` ops
// the spiller already uses for float values — which is exactly how arm64 preserves d8–d15
// (`Arm64PrologueEpilogue`, "THE CALLEE-SAVED REGISTERS ARE SPILLED TO FRAME SLOTS"). Reusing
// the slot ops rather than minting a 16-byte save op keeps the `TargetOp` contract frozen and
// makes the two targets ONE mechanism rather than two.
//
// ⚠⚠ EIGHT BYTES PER XMM IS SUFFICIENT, AND THAT IS A LIVE PRECONDITION RATHER THAN A SHORTCUT.
// Win64 nominally obliges a callee to preserve all 128 bits of xmm6–15, and a slot store of an XMM
// emits `movsd`, which saves only the low 64. The compiler also has no type wider than 64 bits and never
// reads bits 64–127 of an XMM, so the low half IS the value. This is the same latitude AAPCS64 gives
// arm64 for d8–d15, which is why its 8-byte slots are correct there too.
//
// The 128-bit obligation is owed to an EXTERNAL caller of a compiler function, and the enumeration of
// those is the live precondition — so it is stated exactly rather than as a slogan. The compiler exports no
// DLL entry and its only OUTBOUND boundary is `iatCall` into kernel32, where the compiler is the CALLER and
// the callee preserves the compiler's registers. It does hand the OS **three inbound entry points** of its
// own, and each is safe for a stated reason rather than by assumption:
//   • `mrt_fault_thunk` — a **Vectored Exception Handler** (X64Runtime), and its Linux SIGFPE twin.
//     Both run only on the way to `mrt_panic` and terminate the process; neither returns to the OS,
//     so no register state is owed back.
// ⇒ **IF the compiler EVER GAINS A VECTOR TYPE WIDER THAN 64 BITS, OR EVER HANDS THE OS A CALLBACK THAT
// RETURNS AND MAY TOUCH AN XMM, THIS MUST WIDEN TO 16 BYTES** (a `movups` pair and a 16-byte slot
// stride). There is exactly one number to change — `XmmSaveSlotBytes` below — and every frame that
// preserves an XMM reads it, so widening it and forgetting the GT half is a compile-time panic
// (`assertGtFrameConsistent`, X64GtRuntime) rather than the upper 64 bits of xmm6–15 quietly dying
// across every `await`.
//
// ⭐ WHY A FRAME POINTER, AND WHY ON EVERY FUNCTION INCLUDING LEAVES
//
// The frame pointer's job is not addressing — every local and spill slot here is
// addressed off `rsp` (see `spillSlotBaseDisp`), and that does not change. Its job is
// the SAVED-RBP CHAIN: `[rbp]` is the caller's rbp and `[rbp+8]` is the return address,
// so the frames of a live thread form a linked list that can be WALKED. Two things need
// exactly that list, and neither can be served by anything cheaper:
//
//   1. The PANIC BACKTRACE (here). `mrt_panic_walk_frames` follows the chain to
//      symbolize the callers of a fault or a panic. Frame 0 comes from the faulting RIP,
//      but every frame above it comes from the chain.
//   2. GROWABLE GREEN-THREAD STACKS. A Go-style `morestack` allocates a larger stack,
//      COPIES the old one onto it, and must then find and fix every interior frame
//      pointer — which have all just moved. The chain is HOW it finds them:
//      `__gt_morestack` walks it and adds the relocation offset to each saved rbp. A
//      relocating stack without a frame-pointer chain has no way to enumerate the
//      pointers it just invalidated.
//
// So the chain is needed whether or not stack traces are.
//
// LEAVES ARE INCLUDED, and that is the point rather than an oversight: a frameless leaf
// cannot be unwound OUT of, so it terminates any walk that reaches it — and the faulting
// `main` of `specs/safety.md` IS a frameless leaf. A chain with a hole in it is not a
// chain. There is therefore no `needsFrame` test: the frame POINTER is
// unconditional, while the frame RESERVATION (`sub rsp, N`) is still elided when there is
// nothing to reserve.
//
// It costs NO register. `allocatablePool` excludes rsp/rbp ("the frame"), so a frame pointer's
// full price — 14 allocatable GPRs, not 15 — is paid whether or not rbp holds a chain.
//
// The runtime entry stub `mrt_start` builds its own frame directly in
// `augmentWithRuntime` (which runs AFTER this pass), so this pass never sees it — it
// sets up the same `push rbp` / `mov rbp, rsp` by hand, because the chain has to start
// somewhere and a trace that cannot name `mrt_start` stops one frame short.
//
// ⭐ THE FRAME-POINTER-FIRST ORDERING ALSO FIXES WHERE INCOMING STACK ARGUMENTS ARE. `mov rbp, rsp`
// runs BEFORE any callee-saved push, so `[rbp + 16]` is the caller's rsp-at-the-call whatever this
// function's frame size or push count turns out to be — which is what lets the ISEL compute an
// incoming stack argument's displacement (`win64IncomingArgDisp`) long before the allocator decides
// either. Push the callee-saved registers ahead of the frame pointer and every one of those
// displacements silently gains `8 * pushCount`. And because EVERY function is framed, no guard for
// a frameless leaf with stack parameters is needed — there is no such leaf.

// `push rbp` is a push like any other, and the frame arithmetic is the one place that has
// to know it: it moves rsp by 8 exactly as a callee-saved push does, so it counts toward
// the parity that decides how much `sub rsp` must reserve to land 16-aligned AT the call.
// Named rather than folded into a `+ 1`, because a bare 1 in that expression is exactly
// the magic value that makes an alignment bug invisible.
let FramePointerPushCount = 1 as RegCount

// The return address a `call` pushes below its caller's frame.
let CallReturnAddressWords = 1 as RegCount

// Win64 requires a caller to reserve 32 bytes of SHADOW SPACE below its outgoing args
// — scratch the callee may use for its first four register parameters. Every function
// that makes a call reserves it, `mrt_start`'s hand-built 0x20 frame included.
export let ShadowSpaceBytes = 32 as StackSize

// ⛔⛔ **THE BYTES BELOW RSP A FUNCTION MAY KEEP LIVE DATA IN — System V's "red zone", which Win64 does not
// have and this compiler therefore never uses.** Everything a frame holds is at or above RSP, which is what
// makes the memory below it free for somebody else to write.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ASYNCHRONOUS PREEMPTION IS THAT SOMEBODY ELSE.** Stopping a thread pushes the interrupted PC at
// `rsp - 8` of the thread's own stack, so a non-zero red zone would silently overwrite a live value of the
// interrupted function at every stop. `X64GtRuntime.buildPreemptMachineChunk` REFUSES to build against a
// non-zero one rather than relying on this line being read.
export let X64RedZoneBytes = 0 as StackSize

// The custom-ABI callee-saved GPRs, in PUSH order (popped in reverse). The register
// allocator may color values into any of these; the prologue pushes exactly the ones a
// function's coloring used, and the epilogue pops them. rbx + r12–r15 mirror
// RegisterAllocator.calleeSavedMask (kept in one order here so push/pop stay paired).
// EXPORTED so the GT context switch (X64GtRuntime) saves EXACTLY the set the prologue
// pushes — one ordered list, not a second copy free to drift into register corruption
// across a switch.
export let calleeSavedOrder = [X64Register.rbx, X64Register.r12, X64Register.r13, X64Register.r14, X64Register.r15]

// The custom-ABI callee-saved XMMs, in SAVE order. Unlike the GPRs these are not a stack —
// each takes its own frame slot — so the restore order is the same, not the reverse. xmm6–15 mirror
// `RegisterAllocator.calleeSavedMask`'s XMM half, held to it by `assertCalleeSavedListsMatchMasks`.
//
// EXPORTED for the same reason `calleeSavedOrder` is: the GT context switch (X64GtRuntime) must save
// EXACTLY the set the prologue preserves, because a `call __gt_context_switch` is an ordinary call
// from the suspending function's view — and it runs another green thread's arbitrary code before
// returning. Two shared lists, not second copies free to drift into register corruption.
export let calleeSavedXmmOrder = [X64Register.xmm6, X64Register.xmm7, X64Register.xmm8, X64Register.xmm9, X64Register.xmm10, X64Register.xmm11, X64Register.xmm12, X64Register.xmm13, X64Register.xmm14, X64Register.xmm15]

// The frame bytes ONE preserved XMM occupies — **the single home of the "8 bytes per XMM" decision
// this file's header argues for**, read by every frame that preserves one: the prologue's
// callee-saved slots below, the GT context switch's hand-built save area, and `__gt_morestack`'s
// FP-argument save area (both X64GtRuntime).
//
// It IS `SpillSlotBytes` and says so rather than repeating an 8, because a preserved XMM is exactly a
// SPILLED f64: the prologue preserves one with the spiller's own `storeSlotReg`/`loadRegSlot` ops, so
// its slot rides the spiller's grid and its width cannot be anything else.
//
// ⚠ BOTH HALVES READ THIS ONE NAME. A separate GT spelling (the ASYNC ARGUMENT-BUFFER stride, a
// different fact that happens to be 8) would leave `assertGtFrameConsistent` reading only one of them,
// so forgetting the other would be silent. Read from here, widening it to 16 moves that assert's
// derived frame off `GtInitialFrameBytes` and the next async compile panics.
export let XmmSaveSlotBytes = SpillSlotBytes

module typealias X64RegisterArray = Array with X64Register

// Insert prologue/epilogue ops for every function in the module.
export function insertPrologueEpilogue(module TargetModule, target Target)
	logDebug(LogCategory.codegen, message: function() gives "Prologue/epilogue insertion ({targetToString(target)}): {module.funcCount()} function(s)")

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		insertFrameForFunction(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("insertPrologueEpilogue: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'insertPrologueEpilogue'

// Insert a function's frame: establish the frame pointer, push the callee-saved registers
// its coloring used, reserve its frame (`sub rsp`), and mirror all three on every return.
//
// EVERY function is framed, leaves included — the saved-RBP chain is only a chain if it has
// no holes (see this file's header). The frame RESERVATION is still conditional: a leaf that
// reserves nothing emits no `sub rsp`, since `computeFrameBytes` returns 0 and both
// `encodePrologue` and `encodeEpilogue` skip a zero-size frame.
//
// `colouredPhysicalRegs` is read BEFORE anything is inserted, so it never sees this pass's own pushes.
function insertFrameForFunction(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	let coloured = colouredPhysicalRegs(module, func: func)
	let usedCallee = usedRegsFrom(calleeSavedOrder, coloured: coloured)
	let usedXmm = usedRegsFrom(calleeSavedXmmOrder, coloured: coloured)
	let makesCall = functionMakesCall(module, func: func)

	// The SAME `computeFrameBytes` the GT stack guard reaches through `x64PrologueFrameOf` — one formula, two
	// callers, so the guard's projected rsp and this `sub rsp, N` can never disagree.
	let frameBytes = computeFrameBytes(func, makesCall: makesCall, pushCount: usedCallee.count(), xmmSaveCount: usedXmm.count())

	// The callee-saved XMM slots sit directly above the allocator's own spill slots, on the same grid.
	let xmmBaseSlot = func.stackSize / SpillSlotBytes

	var isFirstBlock = true

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("insertPrologueEpilogue: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		if isFirstBlock 'entry'
			insertPrologueAtEntry(module, block: block, usedCallee: usedCallee, usedXmm: usedXmm, xmmBaseSlot: xmmBaseSlot as SpillSlot, frameBytes: frameBytes)
		end 'entry'

		isFirstBlock = false

		if blockEndsWithReturn(module, block: block) 'returnBlock'
			appendEpilogueBeforeReturn(module, block: block, usedCallee: usedCallee, usedXmm: usedXmm, xmmBaseSlot: xmmBaseSlot as SpillSlot, frameBytes: frameBytes)
		end 'returnBlock'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'insertFrameForFunction'

// What a function's prologue lays below `push rbp`: the callee-saved GPRs its coloring used, and the frame
// `sub rsp, N` reserves under them.
module type X64PrologueFrame
	export var pushCount as RegCount
	export var frameBytes as StackSize

	export static function create(pushCount RegCount, frameBytes StackSize) returns X64PrologueFrame
		return Self{pushCount: pushCount, frameBytes: frameBytes}
	end 'create'
end 'X64PrologueFrame'

// ⭐ **THE PROLOGUE's SHAPE, READ OFF THE COLORING ONCE** — the `computeFrameBytes` expression
// `insertFrameForFunction` uses inline, so the GT stack guard (X64Backend's `emitFunctionChunk`, which projects rsp
// by `frameBytes`) and the preemption certificate (`x64CallStackBytes`) cannot size one function two ways.
module function x64PrologueFrameOf(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns X64PrologueFrame
	let makesCall = functionMakesCall(module, func: func)
	let coloured = colouredPhysicalRegs(module, func: func)
	let pushCount = usedRegsFrom(calleeSavedOrder, coloured: coloured).count()
	let xmmSaveCount = usedRegsFrom(calleeSavedXmmOrder, coloured: coloured).count()
	return X64PrologueFrame.create(pushCount, frameBytes: computeFrameBytes(func, makesCall: makesCall, pushCount: pushCount, xmmSaveCount: xmmSaveCount))
end 'x64PrologueFrameOf'

// Everything one call of `func` puts on the stack below its caller's frame: the return address, the frame pointer
// and callee-saved pushes, and the frame reserved under them. EXPORTED for
// `PreemptionDepth.requirePreemptionFitsTheGuardMargin`, which adds a call tree up from these.
export function x64CallStackBytes(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns StackSize
	let frame = x64PrologueFrameOf(module, func: func)
	let wordsBelowTheCall = CallReturnAddressWords + FramePointerPushCount + frame.pushCount
	return (wordsBelowTheCall * StackWordBytes) as StackSize + frame.frameBytes
end 'x64CallStackBytes'

// The bytes to reserve with `sub rsp, N`.
//   • A CALLER must leave rsp 16-byte aligned AT the call. Entry rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16) — the
//     `call` that reached us pushed the return address. Every push then moves it by 8, and
//     the `sub` must land it on ≡ 0. The pushes are `push rbp` PLUS the `pushCount`
//     callee-saved ones, which is the whole reason the frame pointer appears in this
//     arithmetic: it is one more push, so it INVERTS the parity the reservation must
//     correct for. It must also cover shadow space + locals + this function's OUTGOING
//     stack arguments (`func.outgoingArgSize`, the max over its call sites — 0 for a function
//     whose every call fits the six register slots of each file).
//     `mrt_start`'s hand-built frame IS this formula with pushCount 0, stackSize 0.
//   • A LEAF has no call, so alignment is irrelevant; it reserves just its (16-rounded)
//     locals — 0 for the leaves that have none. push/pop alone balance rsp.
//
// ⚠ `xmmSaveCount` FOLDS INTO THE RESERVATION, NOT INTO `pushCount`. The callee-saved XMMs live in
// frame SLOTS, not on the push stack (there is no `push xmm`), so they do not move rsp's residue and
// must not be counted in the parity term — `pushCount` stays the GPR push count. Counting them there
// would flip the reservation's residue for every function saving an odd number of XMMs and misalign
// rsp at its calls, which is not a compile error but a fault inside kernel32's SSE.
//
// ⚠ A LEAF SAVES CALLEE-SAVED XMMs TOO, and its branch must reserve them: "leaf" means "makes no
// call", not "is not a callee". `preferredVolatilityMask` puts a float into xmm6–15
// under plain register pressure in a function that calls nothing, and that register still belongs to
// this function's caller.
function computeFrameBytes(func IrFunction, makesCall bool, pushCount RegCount, xmmSaveCount RegCount) returns StackSize
	let xmmSaveBytes = (xmmSaveCount as StackSize) * XmmSaveSlotBytes

	if not makesCall 'leaf'
		return alignUpStack(func.stackSize + xmmSaveBytes, alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
	end 'leaf'

	let base = func.stackSize + xmmSaveBytes + func.outgoingArgSize + ShadowSpaceBytes
	let targetMod = frameAlignTarget(pushCount + FramePointerPushCount)
	return roundUpToMod(base, targetMod: targetMod, modulus: StackFrameAlignment)
end 'computeFrameBytes'

// The rsp offset of a function's spill-slot AREA — the base the x64 encoder adds to
// `slot * SpillSlotBytes` for every spill/reload. The frame the prologue reserves is laid
// out, from rsp upward: [shadow space + outgoing args] [spill slots] [callee-saved XMM slots]
// [alignment padding]. The XMM slots ride the SAME grid as the spiller's, which is why they need no
// base of their own — the layout is decided here once, for both.
// So spill slots must sit ABOVE the shadow space on a CALLER: at a `call`, the callee may
// clobber the caller's 32-byte shadow space (`[rsp .. rsp+32)`), which would corrupt a
// value spilled across a loop whose body makes a call. Placing the slot area at
// `ShadowSpaceBytes + outgoingArgSize` keeps every slot out of that clobber zone. A LEAF
// (no call) reserves no shadow space, so its slots start at rsp (base 0) — the simplest
// form, matching the design's "slot disp = slotIndex*8 from rsp". This is the SINGLE place
// the shadow-space-vs-slot ordering is decided; `computeFrameBytes` reserves exactly enough
// (`stackSize + outgoingArgSize + ShadowSpaceBytes`) for a caller to hold both.
export function spillSlotBaseDisp(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns StackSize
	if functionMakesCall(module, func: func) 'caller'
		return ShadowSpaceBytes + func.outgoingArgSize
	end 'caller'

	return 0
end 'spillSlotBaseDisp'

// The `sub rsp` size's required residue mod 16 for a caller with `pushCount` TOTAL pushes
// (the frame pointer's own included — see `computeFrameBytes`): 8 when even (entry rsp ≡ 8
// stays ≡ 8 after an even number of pushes, so an 8 (mod 16) sub reaches ≡ 0), 0 when odd.
function frameAlignTarget(pushCount RegCount) returns StackSize
	if pushCount mod 2 == 0 'even'
		return 8
	end 'even'

	return 0
end 'frameAlignTarget'

// The smallest value >= `base` congruent to `targetMod` modulo `modulus` — `base` plus
// the least non-negative delta that lands its residue on `targetMod`.
function roundUpToMod(base StackSize, targetMod StackSize, modulus StackSize) returns StackSize
	// `modulus` is a power-of-two stack alignment, never zero; `otherwise panic` is the project's form
	// for a `try` that must not fail (integer `mod` throws for a possibly-zero divisor).
	let residue = try (base mod modulus) otherwise panic("roundUpToMod: a stack alignment of 0 — it is a power of two")
	let delta = try (((targetMod - residue) + modulus) mod modulus) otherwise panic("roundUpToMod: a stack alignment of 0 — it is a power of two")
	return base + delta
end 'roundUpToMod'

// The registers of `order` that the coloring used, in `order`'s order. The allocator colors values
// into the callee-saved registers of EITHER file for values live across a call (or under pressure);
// each such register must be preserved — a GPR by push/pop, an XMM by a frame slot.
function usedRegsFrom(order X64RegisterArray, coloured RegMask) returns X64RegisterArray
	var used = X64RegisterArray.create()

	for reg in order 'eachCallee'
		if regMaskContains(coloured, regNum: reg.rawValue) 'isUsed'
			used.push(reg)
		end 'isUsed'
	end 'eachCallee'

	return used
end 'usedRegsFrom'

// Prepend the frame-pointer setup, then the callee-saved GPR pushes, then the frame `sub`, then the
// callee-saved XMM slot stores, to the entry block, so at runtime they run in that order. Built by
// inserting at index 0 in REVERSE (the XMM stores first, then the `sub` before them, then each push,
// then the frame pointer before them all), so the final order is:
// push rbp, mov rbp/rsp, push₀ … pushₙ, sub, storeSlotReg₀ … storeSlotRegₘ.
//
// The frame pointer is established FIRST, before any callee-saved push, and that ordering
// is what makes the chain well-formed: at `mov rbp, rsp` the only things below the return
// address are the caller's saved rbp, so `[rbp]` is the caller's frame pointer and
// `[rbp+8]` is the return address — the two loads `mrt_panic_walk_frames` does. Pushing a
// callee-saved register first would bury the return address one slot deeper per push, and
// the walker has no way to know how many.
function insertPrologueAtEntry(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, usedCallee X64RegisterArray, usedXmm X64RegisterArray, xmmBaseSlot SpillSlot, frameBytes StackSize)
	// The XMM stores are prepended FIRST, so they end up LAST — after the `sub rsp` that reserves the
	// slots they write into. They address rsp, so running one before the reservation would store above
	// the stack pointer, into memory a signal or interrupt frame may overwrite.
	for xi in 0 upto usedXmm.count() 'eachXmmSaveRev'
		let slotIndex = usedXmm.count() - 1 - xi
		let reg = try usedXmm.get(slotIndex) otherwise panic("insertPrologueAtEntry: usedXmm.get OOB")
		prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.storeSlotReg(xmmBaseSlot + slotIndex, srcReg: x64PhysReg(reg)))
	end 'eachXmmSaveRev'

	if frameBytes > 0 'reserve'
		prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.prologue(frameBytes))
	end 'reserve'

	for ri in 0 upto usedCallee.count() 'eachPushRev'
		let reg = try usedCallee.get(usedCallee.count() - 1 - ri) otherwise panic("insertPrologueAtEntry: usedCallee.get OOB")
		prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.pushReg(reg))
	end 'eachPushRev'

	prependOp(module, block: block, op: framePointerSetupOp())
	prependOp(module, block: block, op: TargetOp.pushReg(X64Register.rbp))
end 'insertPrologueAtEntry'

// Append the frame teardown before the `ret` terminator, mirroring the prologue exactly in
// reverse: reload the callee-saved XMMs (still inside the frame that holds their slots), release
// the frame (`add rsp`), pop the callee-saved GPRs in REVERSE push order (LIFO), then restore the
// caller's frame pointer. Appended to the body, which runs before the `ret` terminator.
//
// The XMM reloads are NOT reversed, and need not be: each register has its OWN slot rather than a
// stack position, so save and restore order are independent. Only the pushes are LIFO, a stack being
// the one thing here that is one.
//
// `pop rbp` is reached with rsp back at the value `mov rbp, rsp` captured, because the
// `add rsp` and the pops undo the `sub` and the pushes one for one. Spelling the epilogue
// `mov rsp, rbp; pop rbp` would restore rsp from the frame pointer rather than by balanced
// arithmetic, but that form CANNOT also restore the callee-saved registers, which sit below rbp and
// would be skipped over: they are pushed after the frame pointer, so they are unwound explicitly
// and the balance is what carries rsp home.
function appendEpilogueBeforeReturn(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, usedCallee X64RegisterArray, usedXmm X64RegisterArray, xmmBaseSlot SpillSlot, frameBytes StackSize)
	for xi in 0 upto usedXmm.count() 'eachXmmRestore'
		let reg = try usedXmm.get(xi) otherwise panic("appendEpilogueBeforeReturn: usedXmm.get OOB")
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.loadRegSlot(x64PhysReg(reg), slot: xmmBaseSlot + xi))
	end 'eachXmmRestore'

	if frameBytes > 0 'release'
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.epilogue(frameBytes))
	end 'release'

	for ri in 0 upto usedCallee.count() 'eachPopRev'
		let reg = try usedCallee.get(usedCallee.count() - 1 - ri) otherwise panic("appendEpilogueBeforeReturn: usedCallee.get OOB")
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.popReg(reg))
	end 'eachPopRev'

	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.popReg(X64Register.rbp))
end 'appendEpilogueBeforeReturn'

// `mov rbp, rsp` — the instruction that makes rbp name this frame. Reuses the existing
// 64-bit reg-reg move rather than minting a TargetOp variant for it: the bytes are an
// ordinary `mov r/m64, r64`, and a new variant would have to be band-appended for no
// encoding it does not already have. Shared by this pass and `mrt_start`'s hand-built
// frame, so the two cannot spell the frame's defining instruction differently.
export function framePointerSetupOp() returns TargetOp
	return TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rbp), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.rsp))
end 'framePointerSetupOp'

// Insert `op` at the front of `block`'s op list (a fresh `module.ops` slot). The shared
// "prepend a new op" helper the prologue's pushes and `sub` both use.
function prependOp(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, op TargetOp)
	let opIdx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(op)

	// Frame setup is the ABI's, not the author's — it precedes the function's first statement.
	module.spans.pushNone(opIdx)
	insertOpRef(block, at: 0, opIdx: opIdx)
end 'prependOp'

// True when the function makes a call — the frame trigger that forces 16-byte alignment
// + shadow space even for a leaf-caller with no locals. `isCall` is read straight off
// each op's TargetOpMeta backing.
function functionMakesCall(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns bool
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("functionMakesCall: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			if opAtIsCall(module, opIdx: opIdx) 'call'
				return true
			end 'call'
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			if opAtIsCall(module, opIdx: block.terminatorIndex()) 'callTerm'
				return true
			end 'callTerm'
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return false
end 'functionMakesCall'

function opAtIsCall(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("functionMakesCall: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
	return op.rawValue.isCall
end 'opAtIsCall'

// True when the block's terminator is a `ret` — the site an `epilogue` precedes.
function blockEndsWithReturn(module TargetModule, block IrBlock) returns bool
	if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
		return false
	end 'noTerm'

	let op = try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("blockEndsWithReturn: terminator not in module.ops")
	return match op 'check'
		prologue or
			epilogue gives false
		ret gives true
		jmp or
			jcc or
			movRegImm or
			movRegImm32 or
			movRegReg or
			movReg32Reg32 or
			subRegReg or
			imulRegReg or
			negReg or
			cmpRegReg or
			leaRegRegReg or
			leaRegRegImm32 or
			imulRegRegImm32 or
			cmpRegImm32 or
			xchgRegReg or
			callDirect or
			iatCall or
			cqo or
			divideReg gives false
		pushReg or
			popReg gives false
		storeSlotReg or
			loadRegSlot gives false
		andRegReg or
			orRegReg or
			xorRegReg or
			notReg or
			andRegImm32 or
			orRegImm32 or
			xorRegImm32 or
			shlRegImm8 or
			sarRegImm8 or
			shrRegImm8 or
			shlRegCl or
			sarRegCl or
			shrRegCl or
			setccReg or
			leaRegGlobal or
			loadRegBaseDisp or
			storeBaseDispReg or
			addsdRegReg or
			subsdRegReg or
			mulsdRegReg or
			divsdRegReg or
			ucomisdRegReg or
			movsdRegRip or
			cvtsi2sdRegReg or
			cvttsd2siRegReg or
			sqrtsdRegReg or
			roundsdRegReg or
			movqGprXmm or
			absF64RegReg or
			leaRegRdata or
			minMaxSdRegReg or
			movqXmmGpr or
			negF64RegReg gives false

		// Unreachable this rung (arm64 uses Arm64PrologueEpilogue, wave 1). `gives` cannot host a
		// `panic`; wave 1 answers `arm64Ret` true here when it wires the arm64 return block.
		arm64Ret or
			arm64Branch or
			arm64CondBranch or
			arm64Cbz or
			arm64Cbnz or
			arm64MovImm or
			arm64Prologue or
			arm64Epilogue or
			arm64Add or
			arm64Sub or
			arm64Mul or
			arm64Sdiv or
			arm64Udiv or
			arm64Msub or
			arm64And or
			arm64Orr or
			arm64Eor or
			arm64Lslv or
			arm64Asrv or
			arm64Lsrv or
			arm64Neg or
			arm64Mvn or
			arm64AddImm or
			arm64SubImm or
			arm64AndImm or
			arm64OrrImm or
			arm64EorImm or
			arm64LslImm or
			arm64AsrImm or
			arm64LsrImm or
			arm64Cmp or
			arm64CmpImm or
			arm64Cset or
			arm64LeaFuncAddr or
			arm64LeaRdata or
			arm64LeaGlobal or
			arm64BranchLink or
			arm64BranchLinkReg or
			arm64Fadd or
			arm64Fsub or
			arm64Fmul or
			arm64Fdiv or
			arm64Fcmp or
			arm64Scvtf or
			arm64Fcvtzs or
			arm64Fsqrt or
			arm64Frint or
			arm64Fabs or
			arm64FpLoadRdata or
			arm64ImportCall or
			arm64Syscall or
			arm64Fcsel gives false

		// `leaRegFunc`/`callReg` and the Linux `x64Syscall` are body ops, never a `ret`.
		leaRegFunc or
			callReg or
			x64Syscall gives false

		// The jump tables are branch terminators, not a `ret`.
		x64JumpTable or
			arm64JumpTable gives false

		// The witness call is a body op, never a `ret`.
		callMem gives false

		// Both stack-argument ops are body ops, never a `ret`.
		arm64StoreOutgoingArg or
			arm64LoadIncomingArg gives false

		// Both arm64 bit reinterpretations are body ops, never a `ret`.
		arm64FmovGprFp or
			arm64FmovFpGpr gives false

		// A frame address is a body op, never a `ret`.
		leaRegSlot gives false

		// A fill ladder is a body op, never a `ret` — its branches are internal to its own encoding.
		x64MemFill or
			arm64MemFill gives false

		// Every atomic is a body op, never a `ret`.
		x64LockCmpxchg or
			x64LockXadd or
			x64LockAdd or
			x64TebSelfPointer or
			x64GsLoadRegBase or
			arm64AtomicCas or
			arm64AtomicRmw or
			arm64ThreadPointer gives false

		// Every scaled-index form is a body op, never a `ret`.
		leaRegBaseIndexScale or
			loadRegBaseIndexScale or
			storeBaseIndexScaleReg or
			arm64AddLsl gives false

		// The high multiply is a body op, never a `ret`.
		imulHighReg gives false

		// arm64 `fneg` is a body op, never a `ret`.
		arm64Fneg gives false

		// An ordered access is a body op, never a `ret`.
		arm64LoadAcquire or
			arm64StoreRelease gives false

		// The arm64 high multiply is a body op, never a `ret`.
		arm64Smulh gives false

		// A coverage counter is a body op, never a `ret`.
		covInc gives false

		// So is a read-modify-write of memory.
		x64AluBaseDispReg or
			x64AluBaseDispImm32 gives false
	end 'check'
end 'blockEndsWithReturn'
